<Base title="Density Matrices — Shyam Prasad Rao" description="The density matrix generalizes the state vector — populations and coherence, pure versus mixed, the partial trace, and the road to quantum channels.">
  <div class="crumbs"><a href="/atlas">atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span>quantum information<span class="sep">/</span>density matrices</div>

  <article class="post">
    <h1>Density Matrices</h1>
    <div class="it-meta"><span class="pill pub">&#9679; published</span><span>last tended Aug 30, 2026</span><span class="tag">Quantum</span><span>9 min</span></div>

    <h2><span class="qnum">Q1</span>What is it?</h2>
    <p>A density matrix is a second way to write down a quantum state &mdash; and a strictly more general one. For a pure state |&psi;&#10217; it is the outer product of the state with itself:</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho; = |&psi;&#10217;&#10216;&psi;|</div>
    <p>Written out for |&psi;&#10217; = &alpha;|0&#10217; + &beta;|1&#10217;, that is a 2&times;2 matrix:</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho; = [ |&alpha;|&sup2;&nbsp;&nbsp;&alpha;&beta;* ; &alpha;*&beta;&nbsp;&nbsp;|&beta;|&sup2; ]</div>
    <p>The two entries on the diagonal are the measurement probabilities from the <a href="/atlas/measurement">Born rule</a> &mdash; the <strong>populations</strong> of |0&#10217; and |1&#10217;. The two off the diagonal are new: they carry the <strong>coherence</strong> between the basis states, which is where the relative phase has gone. That split is the whole point of the object:</p>
    <div class="formula">diagonal &rarr; populations <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> off-diagonal &rarr; coherence</div>
    <p>Not every matrix is a state. Three conditions make one physical: &rho; must be <strong>Hermitian</strong> (&rho;&#8224; = &rho;, so probabilities come out real), <strong>positive semidefinite</strong> (&#10216;&phi;|&rho;|&phi;&#10217; &ge; 0, so no negative probabilities), and <strong>unit trace</strong> (Tr &rho; = 1, so they sum to one). Anything satisfying all three is a legitimate quantum state; anything violating one is not.</p>
    <p>The payoff is that this description covers states a vector cannot write at all. A state vector always describes a system you know exactly. A density matrix can also describe one you know only statistically &mdash; and, as Q3 shows, one that is genuinely indefinite because it is entangled with something else.</p>

    <h2><span class="qnum">Q2</span>Why does it exist?</h2>
    <p>Because of one distinction that state vectors cannot express. Consider two very different laboratory situations.</p>
    <p><strong>Situation A.</strong> I hand you a qubit in the superposition</p>
    <div class="formula">|+&#10217; = (|0&#10217; + |1&#10217;)/&radic;2</div>
    <p><strong>Situation B.</strong> I flip a fair coin out of your sight and hand you |0&#10217; or |1&#10217; accordingly. You have a qubit; I have a piece of paper saying which.</p>
    <p>Measure either one in the computational basis and you get 0 half the time and 1 half the time. The statistics are identical. But these are not the same state &mdash; apply a Hadamard first and A gives |0&#10217; every time while B still gives a coin flip. Situation A is a <em>superposition</em>; situation B is a <em>classical mixture</em>. State vectors have no way to write B at all: there is no |&psi;&#10217; that means &ldquo;half of one, half of the other, and I mean it classically.&rdquo; Density matrices write both, and the difference is visible on sight:</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho;<sub>pure</sub>&nbsp;&nbsp;= &frac12; [ 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--mark)">1</span> ; <span style="color:var(--mark)">1</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 ] <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;&larr; coherence in the corners</span><br/>&rho;<sub>mixed</sub> = &frac12; [ 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--mark)">0</span> ; <span style="color:var(--mark)">0</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 ] <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;&larr; no coherence at all</span></div>
    <p>Same diagonal &mdash; same computational-basis statistics, as promised. Different off-diagonal: the superposition has coherence, the coin flip has none. <strong>The off-diagonal terms are the mathematical difference between a quantum superposition and mere ignorance</strong>, and that sentence is the reason this node exists.</p>

    <h3>Reading the entries: one qubit</h3>
    <p>The 50/50 case is clean but symmetric, which hides the rule. Take a lopsided state instead:</p>
    <div class="formula">|&psi;&#10217; = ( &radic;3 |0&#10217; + |1&#10217; ) / 2 <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;so&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> &alpha; = &radic;3/2 , &beta; = 1/2</div>
    <p>Its density matrix &rho; = |&psi;&#10217;&#10216;&psi;| has entries indexed by the basis states &mdash; row and column both run over |0&#10217; and |1&#10217;:</p>
    <div class="mtx">
      <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>|0&#10217;</th><th>|1&#10217;</th></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;0|</th><td class="pop">3/4</td><td class="coh">&radic;3/4</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;1|</th><td class="coh">&radic;3/4</td><td class="pop">1/4</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="mtx-key"><span class="kp">&#9632;</span> diagonal &mdash; P(0) = 3/4, P(1) = 1/4 &nbsp;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="kc">&#9632;</span> off-diagonal &mdash; coherence between |0&#10217; and |1&#10217;</div>
    </div>
    <p>Read straight down the diagonal and you have the measurement probabilities, no calculation required: 3/4 and 1/4, summing to 1 because Tr&nbsp;&rho; = 1. The two entries off the diagonal are &alpha;&beta;* = &radic;3/4 &asymp; 0.43 &mdash; not a probability of anything, and not observable in this basis. Now let the qubit sit in a noisy environment until its phase is scrambled:</p>
    <div class="mtx">
      <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>|0&#10217;</th><th>|1&#10217;</th></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;0|</th><td class="pop">3/4</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;1|</th><td>0</td><td class="pop">1/4</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="mtx-key">same probabilities &mdash; 3/4 and 1/4 &mdash; but the coherence is gone (purity drops from 1 to 5/8)</div>
    </div>
    <p>Measure in the computational basis a million times and these two states are indistinguishable. Apply a Hadamard first and they are not. <strong>Decoherence is the off-diagonal entries going to zero while the diagonal sits still</strong> &mdash; which is why T&#8322; is quoted separately from T&#8321; on a hardware spec sheet.</p>

    <h3>The same reading: two qubits</h3>
    <p>Nothing changes with more qubits except the size of the grid. Two qubits have four basis states, so &rho; is 4&times;4, indexed by |00&#10217;, |01&#10217;, |10&#10217;, |11&#10217;. Take the Bell state:</p>
    <div class="formula">|&Phi;&#8314;&#10217; = ( |00&#10217; + |11&#10217; ) / &radic;2</div>
    <div class="mtx">
      <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>|00&#10217;</th><th>|01&#10217;</th><th>|10&#10217;</th><th>|11&#10217;</th></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;00|</th><td class="pop">1/2</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td class="coh">1/2</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;01|</th><td>0</td><td class="pop">0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;10|</th><td>0</td><td>0</td><td class="pop">0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;11|</th><td class="coh">1/2</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td class="pop">1/2</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="mtx-key"><span class="kp">&#9632;</span> diagonal &mdash; P(00) = P(11) = 1/2, P(01) = P(10) = 0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="kc">&#9632;</span> corners &mdash; coherence between |00&#10217; and |11&#10217;</div>
    </div>
    <p>The diagonal says exactly what you would expect from a Bell pair: measure both qubits and you get 00 or 11 with equal probability, never 01 or 10. The corners are the entanglement. Now compare a state that is <em>correlated but not entangled</em> &mdash; I flip a coin and prepare either |00&#10217; or |11&#10217;:</p>
    <div class="mtx">
      <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>|00&#10217;</th><th>|01&#10217;</th><th>|10&#10217;</th><th>|11&#10217;</th></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;00|</th><td class="pop">1/2</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;01|</th><td>0</td><td class="pop">0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;10|</th><td>0</td><td>0</td><td class="pop">0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th class="rowh">&#10216;11|</th><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td class="pop">1/2</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="mtx-key">identical diagonal &mdash; identical measurement statistics &mdash; but no corners, so no entanglement</div>
    </div>
    <p>Both states give perfectly correlated outcomes. Only one of them violates a Bell inequality, and the difference lives entirely in two corner entries. So the rule reads the same at any size:</p>
    <div class="formula">down the diagonal &rarr; what a measurement will give you<br/>off the diagonal &rarr; what is still quantum</div>
    <p>There is a number that compresses this. <strong>Purity</strong> is</p>
    <div class="formula">Tr(&rho;&sup2;) = 1 <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;pure&nbsp;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> Tr(&rho;&sup2;) &lt; 1 <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;mixed&nbsp;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> Tr(&rho;&sup2;) = &frac12; <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;maximally mixed</span></div>
    <p>and it turns &ldquo;how quantum is this state still?&rdquo; into a single scalar you can track as a system decoheres.</p>

    <div class="plate">
