        <text class="dim" x="150" y="200" text-anchor="end">I/2</text>
        <line class="ax" x1="152" y1="196" x2="194" y2="181"/>

        <text x="192" y="26" text-anchor="middle">|0&#10217;</text>
        <text x="192" y="342" text-anchor="middle">|1&#10217;</text>
        <text class="dim" x="200" y="368" text-anchor="middle">surface = pure &middot; interior = mixed &middot; centre = no information</text>
      </svg>
      <div class="plate-caption">fig. 1 &mdash; the Bloch <em>ball</em>. The sphere from the qubit page was only its skin; decoherence is the vector shrinking inward.</div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:40px"><span class="qnum">Q3</span>How does it connect?</h2>
    <p>Three pieces of machinery hang off this node, and each one replaces something narrower.</p>
    <p><strong>Measurement gets simpler, not harder.</strong> The Born rule in operator form was p&#7522; = &#10216;&psi;|P&#7522;|&psi;&#10217;, which needs a state vector. In density-matrix language it becomes a trace:</p>
    <div class="formula">p&#7522; = Tr(&rho; P&#7522;)</div>
    <p>and this one expression works for pure and mixed states alike &mdash; and, swapping P&#7522; for a POVM element E&#7522;, for any measurement whatsoever. One formula now covers the whole of the <a href="/atlas/measurement">measurement</a> node.</p>
    <p><strong>The geometry gets an interior.</strong> Any single-qubit density matrix can be written with three real numbers:</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho; = &frac12; ( I + r&#8407; &middot; &sigma;&#8407; ) <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;where&nbsp;&nbsp;</span> &sigma;&#8407; = (X, Y, Z)</div>
    <p>The Bloch vector r&#8407; has |r&#8407;| = 1 exactly for pure states and |r&#8407;| &lt; 1 for mixed ones, with r&#8407; = 0 &mdash; the centre &mdash; being &rho; = I/2, the state that carries no information at all. The Bloch <em>sphere</em> of the qubit page was only the surface of a solid ball, and fig. 1 is what was hiding inside it.</p>
    <p><strong>Subsystems become describable.</strong> Given a joint state &rho;<sub>AB</sub>, the <strong>partial trace</strong> extracts what A alone looks like:</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho;<sub>A</sub> = Tr<sub>B</sub>( &rho;<sub>AB</sub> )</div>
    <p>And here is the result that makes this node genuinely surprising rather than merely convenient. Take the Bell state from Q2 &mdash; a perfectly known, perfectly pure joint state, purity 1. Trace out either qubit and you get</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho;<sub>A</sub> = &rho;<sub>B</sub> = I/2</div>
    <p>&mdash; the maximally mixed state, the centre of the ball, the state of maximum ignorance. The whole is pure and the parts are random. That is not a limitation of the notation; it is what entanglement <em>is</em>, and no state-vector picture of a single qubit can express it. Mixedness, it turns out, has two sources: classical ignorance about which state was prepared, and quantum entanglement with something you are not looking at. The density matrix cannot tell them apart &mdash; which is itself worth sitting with.</p>

