    <h2 style="margin-top:40px"><span class="qnum">Q4</span>Where is it used?</h2>
    <p>Wherever a quantum system is not perfectly isolated &mdash; which is to say, wherever there is real hardware. An ideal closed system evolves by unitary rotation, and in this language that reads</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho; &rarr; U &rho; U&#8224;</div>
    <p>A real one interacts with an environment it is entangled with and cannot track, so the honest description is the more general map</p>
    <div class="formula">&rho; &rarr; &#8496;(&rho;) <span class="dim">&nbsp;&nbsp;a quantum channel: completely positive, trace preserving</span></div>
    <p>and the machinery for writing those maps down &mdash; CPTP, Kraus operators &mdash; is the next node over. This is also where the coherence terms stop being an abstraction. On a real qubit, <strong>T&#8321;</strong> is the timescale on which the diagonal decays toward equilibrium and <strong>T&#8322;</strong> the timescale on which the off-diagonal terms decay toward zero &mdash; decoherence is literally the off-diagonal entries of &rho; going to zero, the Bloch vector shrinking inward in fig. 1 until nothing quantum is left. Every number on a hardware spec sheet about coherence time is a statement about this matrix.</p>
    <p>Two more homes. <strong>Quantum tomography</strong> exists to reconstruct &rho; from measurement statistics, which is only a coherent goal because &rho; is the complete description of what a real, noisy, possibly-entangled state is. And <strong>error correction</strong> needs mixed states from the start: a corrupted logical qubit is not a vector, and the whole <a href="/atlas/noise-error-correction">Noise &amp; Error Correction</a> map is written in this language.</p>
    <p>To make it concrete: in Qiskit, build a Bell pair, take the partial trace over one qubit, and print the reduced state. Seeing I/2 come back from a state you know exactly is the moment this node stops being notation.</p>

    <h2 class="sec">My open questions</h2>
    <ul class="reading">
      <li>What is the cleanest intuition for the off-diagonal terms &mdash; is there a picture better than &ldquo;the phase information lives here&rdquo;?<span class="src">&mdash; chasing</span></li>
      <li>How exactly does a pure state become mixed through interaction with an environment &mdash; step by step, not just in the limit?<span class="src">&mdash; open</span></li>
      <li>Why does tracing out part of an entangled pure state produce a mixed state? I can compute it; I can't yet feel it.<span class="src">&mdash; the one that nags</span></li>
      <li>What is the physical meaning of complete positivity, as opposed to plain positivity?<span class="src">&mdash; next node</span></li>
      <li>How should I think about the Kraus representation geometrically?<span class="src">&mdash; next node</span></li>
      <li>At what point does it become more useful to think in states and channels than in individual qubits?<span class="src">&mdash; a question about taste, maybe</span></li>
    </ul>

